{# Methodology reference modal — static English content, included on all pages.
   Opened by the footer #methodology-link button, Esc/backdrop/close to dismiss.
   Accessibility mirrors the gate-modal (role=dialog, aria-modal, focus trap). #}
<div class="methodology-overlay" id="methodology-modal" hidden>
  <div class="methodology-modal" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="methodology-title">
    <button type="button" class="methodology-close" id="methodology-close" aria-label="Close">&times;</button>
    {% include "_rotation_illo.html.j2" %}
    <h2 id="methodology-title">How the scanner works</h2>
    <div class="methodology-body">
      <p><strong>In one sentence:</strong> this tool ranks 18 investment themes by
         how strongly they have been outperforming the world stock market lately,
         and refreshes that ranking every day.</p>
      <p>It is analytical tooling, not investment advice. It tells you what has
         been strong. It cannot tell you what will be.</p>

      <h3>Start here: what is a &ldquo;theme&rdquo;?</h3>
      <p>A theme is a group of companies that share one idea &mdash; robotics,
         uranium mining, cybersecurity, gold miners. Each theme here is represented
         by a single <strong>ETF</strong> (exchange-traded fund): one fund you can
         buy that holds all the companies in that theme at once. So instead of
         picking individual stocks, the scanner compares 18 baskets against each
         other.</p>
      <p>The scanner scores US-listed ETFs, because they have the longest price
         history and the most trading activity. For each theme it also lists the
         closest <strong>UCITS</strong> equivalent &mdash; a European-domiciled fund
         that a European investor can actually buy. Open any row to see it. Those
         are reference only; the scoring always uses the US fund.</p>

      <h3>The idea it is built on: momentum</h3>
      <p><strong>Momentum</strong> is the observation that things which have been
         going up relative to the market often keep going up for a while &mdash; and
         things that have been lagging often keep lagging. It is one of the
         longest-studied patterns in markets. It is a tendency, not a law: momentum
         fails, sometimes sharply and for long stretches.</p>
      <p>Everything here is <strong>relative</strong>. A theme scores well by beating
         the benchmark, not by going up. In a falling market the top-ranked theme may
         still be losing money &mdash; just less than everything else.</p>

      <h3>What gets measured</h3>
      <p>Each theme is measured on two questions, using price data only:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Level &mdash; how strong is it right now?</strong> Its relative
            strength against the benchmark, its 3-month and 6-month returns, and
            whether its price sits above its own 50-day average.</li>
        <li><strong>Change &mdash; which way is that heading?</strong> Whether the
            relative strength is itself rising, the most recent month&rsquo;s
            return, the slope of that 50-day average, and whether trading volume
            supports the move.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Level without Change is a theme that is strong but possibly peaking. Change
         without Level is one that is still weak but waking up. The scanner cares
         about both, which is why they are weighted equally.</p>

      <h3>Turning that into one number</h3>
      <p>The eight measurements are on completely different scales &mdash; a
         percentage return and a slope cannot be added together. So each one is
         converted to a <strong>z-score</strong>, which simply asks: <em>how unusual
         is this value compared to the other themes today?</em></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>0</strong> = exactly average for the group.</li>
        <li><strong>+1</strong> = one standard deviation above average, roughly
            better than 5 out of 6 themes.</li>
        <li><strong>&minus;1</strong> = the same distance below average.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Because the comparison is always <em>against the other themes on the same
         day</em>, a composite of +1.5 in a strong market and in a weak one mean the
         same thing: this theme is well ahead of its peers. It says nothing about
         whether the group as a whole is doing well.</p>
      <p>The <strong>composite score</strong> is 50% Level + 50% Change, and the
         ranking is simply the composite sorted best to worst.</p>

      <h3>Horizon: how long you intend to hold</h3>
      <p>This is the setting that changes what the dashboard tells you to do.</p>
      <p>Ranks move around every day, often by a place or two, mostly for no
         meaningful reason. If you buy the top 5 and sell the moment something slips
         to 6th, you will trade constantly and pay for the privilege. So the scanner
         uses two thresholds instead of one:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A theme becomes a <strong>buy</strong> candidate when it reaches the top
            few ranks.</li>
        <li>It only becomes a <strong>sell</strong> when it falls well past that
            &mdash; not on the first slip.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The gap between those two thresholds is the <strong>hold band</strong>, and
         it is deliberately silent. A theme drifting inside it is not news. This one
         idea &mdash; keeping what you own until it has clearly fallen out, rather
         than the instant it stops being best &mdash; cuts trading roughly in half
         in testing without giving up return.</p>
      <p>Three horizons are offered. Each shows its historical trading frequency
         next to its return, so the cost of the faster ones is visible:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Short</strong> &mdash; reacts quickest, trades most.</li>
        <li><strong>Medium</strong> &mdash; the default.</li>
        <li><strong>Long</strong> &mdash; holds fewest names longest, trades least.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Switching horizon re-labels the leaderboard instantly. It changes which
         themes are flagged, never the underlying scores or the ranking.</p>

      <h3>Entry, Exit, and the silence between</h3>
      <p>The badges follow directly from the horizon:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>&#9650; Entry</strong> &mdash; inside the buy band.</li>
        <li><strong>no badge</strong> &mdash; inside the hold band. Nothing to do.</li>
        <li><strong>&#9660; Exit</strong> &mdash; it has fallen out of the hold band.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>These describe <em>position</em>, not excitement. A theme collapsing fast
         but still ranked first will read Entry, because by this rule it is still
         one of the strongest things available. The separate <strong>Trend</strong>
         column is what tells you it is deteriorating &mdash; the two are meant to be
         read together.</p>

      <h3>News sentiment (and why it does not count)</h3>
      <p>Each day the scanner collects English news headlines about each theme and
         scores their tone with <strong>FinBERT</strong>, a language model trained on
         financial text. This is shown for interest and is <strong>excluded from the
         ranking</strong>, because headline tone is noisy and often lags the price.
         The leaderboard has an optional toggle to blend it in at a weight you
         choose, but the canonical ranking is pure price data.</p>

      <h3>The backtest, and how much to trust it</h3>
      <p>The Backtest tab replays the strategy over history: at each rebalance date
         it buys the top-ranked themes using only information available on that day,
         holds them under the band rule, and compares the result to the benchmark.</p>
      <p>Read it with three caveats, all of which flatter the strategy:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>It replays <em>today&rsquo;s</em> 18 themes backwards. Several of these
            ETFs did not exist in 2008, so the early years are thinner than they
            look &mdash; and the list was chosen knowing which ideas turned out to
            matter.</li>
        <li>The settings were chosen by testing many combinations on this same
            history. Some of that advantage is fitted to the past and will not
            repeat.</li>
        <li>It assumes you follow the rules exactly, rebalance on schedule, and
            never change your mind.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Use it to compare horizons against each other, which is what it is good at.
         Treat the absolute returns as optimistic.</p>

      <h3>Where the data comes from</h3>
      <p>Daily prices come from yfinance, cached locally so
         each trading day&rsquo;s close is picked up once. News comes from GDELT.
         Macro context in the header shows the S&amp;P 500 versus its 200-day average
         and the VIX volatility index &mdash; both for context only; neither affects
         any score.</p>

      <h3>What this deliberately does not do</h3>
      <p>It does not size positions, account for tax or trading costs in the live
         view, know what you already own, or have any opinion about whether now is a
         good time to invest. It ranks 18 themes by recent relative strength. Every
         decision after that is yours.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<script>
(function () {
  var modal = document.getElementById("methodology-modal");
  if (!modal || modal.dataset.bound) { return; }
  modal.dataset.bound = "1";
  var panel = modal.querySelector('[role="dialog"]');
  var closeBtn = document.getElementById("methodology-close");
  var lastFocus = null;

  function focusables() {
    return panel.querySelectorAll(
      'button, a[href], input, [tabindex]:not([tabindex="-1"])');
  }
  function open() {
    lastFocus = document.activeElement;
    modal.hidden = false;
    (closeBtn || panel).focus();
    document.addEventListener("keydown", onKey);
  }
  function close() {
    modal.hidden = true;
    document.removeEventListener("keydown", onKey);
    if (lastFocus && lastFocus.focus) { lastFocus.focus(); }
  }
  function onKey(e) {
    if (e.key === "Escape") { close(); return; }
    if (e.key === "Tab") {
      var f = focusables();
      if (!f.length) { return; }
      var first = f[0], last = f[f.length - 1];
      if (e.shiftKey && document.activeElement === first) { e.preventDefault(); last.focus(); }
      else if (!e.shiftKey && document.activeElement === last) { e.preventDefault(); first.focus(); }
    }
  }
  modal.addEventListener("click", function (e) { if (e.target === modal) { close(); } });
  if (closeBtn) { closeBtn.addEventListener("click", close); }

  function bindTrigger() {
    var link = document.getElementById("methodology-link");
    if (link) { link.addEventListener("click", function (e) { e.preventDefault(); open(); }); }
    if (location.hash === "#methodology") { open(); }
  }
  if (document.readyState === "loading") {
    document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", bindTrigger);
  } else {
    bindTrigger();
  }
})();
</script>
